The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
if (logger.isWarnEnabled()) {
    logger.warn("Caught exception while allowing TestExecutionListener [" + testExecutionListener +
            "] to process 'after class' callback for test class [" + testClass + "]", ex);
    if (afterTestClassException == null) {
        afterTestClassException = ex;
    }
}
```
Reason:
The assignment of `afterTestClassException` is placed inside the logging condition, which means that if the logger is not enabled for warnings, the exception will not be recorded, leading to untracked exceptions during the 'after class' callback.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (logger.isWarnEnabled()) {
    logger.warn("Caught exception while allowing TestExecutionListener [" + testExecutionListener +
            "] to process 'after class' callback for test class [" + testClass + "]", ex);
}
if (afterTestClassException == null) {
    afterTestClassException = ex;
}
```
Reason:
By moving the assignment of `afterTestClassException` outside of the logging condition, the exception will be recorded regardless of whether the logger is enabled for warnings, ensuring that exceptions occurring during the 'after class' callback are properly tracked.

